1
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:11,680
*eerie music*

2
00:00:17,920 --> 00:00:19,800
*music turns restless*

3
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:22,240
There.

4
00:00:26,080 --> 00:00:28,720
*SA men talking and laughing*

5
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:30,400
*prisoner coughing*

6
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:34,400
*loud gunshot*
*SA men laugh*

7
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,440
*uneasy murmuring*

8
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:38,240
Well?
What is going on?

9
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:41,120
*multiple gunshots ring out*
*man cries out*

10
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:43,040
What are they doing?
(Brock) They'll be here any second.

11
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:44,120
*gunshot rings out*

12
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,400
Make yourself invisible.
Why?

13
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:47,680
(SA Man) One for me!

14
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:49,680
Go on!
- I'm doing it!

15
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:50,920
*gunshot*

16
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:52,720
(SA Man) Again!

17
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:54,680
*man groans in agony*

18
00:00:54,760 --> 00:00:57,240
*SA men laugh*
*man gasps for air*

19
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:02,760
You can take him!
- Clean hit!

20
00:01:02,920 --> 00:01:04,840
*man continues groaning*

21
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:07,760
(Matern) Brock? It's Brock!
- It is Brock.

22
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:12,040
(Matern) I have to get closer.
Hold on, comrade.

23
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:14,520
(Heymann) Brock, we need you.
- (Mandel) Light!

24
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:16,000
*Brock breathes frantically*

25
00:01:16,080 --> 00:01:19,240
I must stop the bleeding.
Easy. Easy now.

26
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:21,960
(Prisoner) Press hard. Harder.
Hang in there!

27
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:24,040
Hey, otherwise he'll bleed to death.
- Harder.

28
00:01:30,840 --> 00:01:32,520
*eerie music*

29
00:01:57,880 --> 00:01:59,520
*rhythmic music*

30
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:16,080
*doorbell rings*

31
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:20,720
Frau Helfers, what a pleasure.

32
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:24,160
Frau Luxemburg.
- Come on in.

33
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:28,560
*she clears her throat*
Is she here?

34
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:32,040
No, but I expect
Dr. Völcker will arrive shortly.

35
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:33,200
Ah.

36
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:37,200
I baked some doughnuts.

37
00:02:39,120 --> 00:02:40,840
My sister's recipe.

38
00:02:46,320 --> 00:02:48,200
(Behnke) Yes, N, as in North Pole,
Natalia, and of course 'Luxenburg'.

39
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:51,720
'Natalia', and of course 'Luxenburg'.

40
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:53,480
Aha, really?

41
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:56,760
I didn't know that.
- It's not important anyway.

42
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:59,880
Only our grandfather,
on my father's side,

43
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:03,160
one of the wealthiest merchants
in the Kingdom of Poland,

44
00:03:03,240 --> 00:03:08,040
he's still turning in his grave today
because his name is no longer spelled right.

45
00:03:08,120 --> 00:03:09,240
*clock ticking*

46
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:21,000
I'll brew another pot.

47
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:27,760
*door opens and closes*

48
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:29,760
*indistinct whispering*

49
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:48,080
(Behnke) I can't keep her here
for hours on end.

50
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:51,480
Tell her about the family,
or about Liebknecht.

51
00:03:51,560 --> 00:03:52,960
I know nothing about him,

52
00:03:53,120 --> 00:03:55,520
except that he was a daredevil.
*doorbell rings*

53
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:57,800
That's her, that must be her.

54
00:03:57,880 --> 00:03:59,320
*Litten cries out*

55
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:05,200
Herr Katelbach? Herr Litten?

56
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:07,760
What kind of charade is this?

57
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:09,520
Who are you?
*doorbell rings*

58
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:11,080
That is surely Dr. Völcker.

59
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:14,600
Come along, Frau Helfers,
we'll await you in the parlor.

60
00:04:15,480 --> 00:04:19,320
Under no circumstances do I wish
to be part of this farce.

61
00:04:19,399 --> 00:04:22,440
Allow us to explain.
- It was the only way.

62
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:25,040
You are two figures of great influence.

63
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:26,960
And open-minded.
- Quiet now!

64
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:28,440
*knocking*

65
00:04:28,520 --> 00:04:29,560
(Völcker) Hello?

66
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Please.

67
00:04:37,080 --> 00:04:39,400
Please. Come in.
- Let me be, please.

68
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:40,920
(whispers) Yes.
- After you.

69
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:44,200
Frau Völcker, what a joy.

70
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:45,720
Comrade, if you don't mind.

71
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:47,880
Comrade Völcker,
of course.

72
00:04:47,960 --> 00:04:50,280
Is Frau Helfers here?
- Yes.

73
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,160
Please, come in.

74
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:00,800
May I?
- No.

75
00:05:11,320 --> 00:05:12,440
Where is Helfers?

76
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:16,520
I'll go... There she is already.

77
00:05:21,120 --> 00:05:22,520
She is no Luxemburg.

78
00:05:22,680 --> 00:05:23,760
I know.

79
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:30,600
Very well.

80
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:34,640
Yet I am Elisabeth Behnke.

81
00:05:34,720 --> 00:05:36,080
*somber music*

82
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:38,680
You may revile me and damn me,

83
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:42,000
yet everything I do and have done,

84
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:45,920
is done with a pure heart
and genuine purpose.

85
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:49,960
Surely we are united by the same goal.

86
00:05:51,440 --> 00:05:53,520
The enemy stands
before his greatest triumph.

87
00:05:54,120 --> 00:05:58,000
Only by your union
can he still be stopped.

88
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:01,600
You can steer the destiny of this country now,

89
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:04,960
or out of false pride

90
00:06:05,480 --> 00:06:07,520
surrender our beloved land
to its destruction.

91
00:06:09,720 --> 00:06:14,200
'Men make their own history,
not of their own free will,

92
00:06:15,240 --> 00:06:17,760
yet they make it themselves.'

93
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:31,560
(whispers) Outstanding!

94
00:06:31,720 --> 00:06:33,360
With a Luxemburg quote.

95
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:35,880
World-class!
You ought to start a party.

96
00:06:35,960 --> 00:06:37,760
They're talking to each other.

97
00:06:39,360 --> 00:06:43,040
Damn it. Do you have another?
- No, mine are all gone too.

98
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:44,560
(Katelbach) I'll take care of that.

99
00:06:44,640 --> 00:06:47,760
Wait, if you're going anyway,
I'll write a grocery list.

100
00:06:47,840 --> 00:06:49,280
Things in there could take a while.

101
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:51,040
You dictate, I'll write.

102
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:56,080
(Newsboy) Berliner Morgenpost!
Berliner Morgenpost!

103
00:06:56,240 --> 00:06:58,720
*typewriters clattering, murmuring*

104
00:07:00,720 --> 00:07:02,680
Chief Inspector,
you wanted to speak with me?

105
00:07:02,760 --> 00:07:04,280
Yes, Miss Ritter.

106
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:12,640
I expect your report by the lunch break.

107
00:07:13,520 --> 00:07:16,280
Which report?
The report on the Forster case

108
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:19,400
and the disappearance
of the suspended Inspector Rath.

109
00:07:20,800 --> 00:07:22,720
Give me Identification.

110
00:07:22,800 --> 00:07:25,840
We should close the book on this case quickly.

111
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:29,800
Yes, Gennat speaking.

112
00:07:29,880 --> 00:07:32,200
One moment.
Excuse me.

113
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:38,080
*measured music*

114
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:40,760
*door opens*

115
00:07:42,840 --> 00:07:44,280
*prisoners coughing*

116
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:45,440
Rath!

117
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:48,720
Rath, Gereon!

118
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:51,920
Step forward!
Here, take this.

119
00:07:53,080 --> 00:07:54,120
Here.

120
00:07:55,760 --> 00:07:56,840
Come along.

121
00:07:58,800 --> 00:08:00,640
*music turns darker*

122
00:08:06,040 --> 00:08:08,040
*cell door is locked*

123
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:16,360
Looks like you and I have to do
the dirty work now.

124
00:08:19,680 --> 00:08:21,600
*music swells dramatically*

125
00:08:27,560 --> 00:08:29,440
*he groans with exertion*

126
00:08:36,840 --> 00:08:38,559
*lamp hums*

127
00:08:43,360 --> 00:08:45,120
*ominous music*

128
00:09:00,520 --> 00:09:02,000
*bell rings, latch clicks*

129
00:09:02,920 --> 00:09:04,080
(Gennat) Come in.

130
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:06,720
*music ends dramatically*

131
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:09,240
My report, Chief Inspector.

132
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:16,520
*he clears his throat*

133
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:26,320
How can you simply drop this investigation?

134
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:29,320
You taught me that we let no one
meddle in our work

135
00:09:29,400 --> 00:09:31,160
in the execution of our duty.

136
00:09:31,240 --> 00:09:33,960
You hammered into us to make no distinction

137
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:35,920
between a corporate director
and a vagrant.

138
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:38,640
You spoke of human dignity
as inviolable.

139
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:40,800
Now you sit behind your desk

140
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:43,360
and abandon one of your finest men?

141
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:45,520
Meanwhile, they are pressuring Böhm

142
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:47,560
because his investigation
threatens the Chancellor.

143
00:09:47,720 --> 00:09:50,240
What on earth is the matter with you,
damn it all?

144
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:56,120
What is this?

145
00:09:56,200 --> 00:09:59,240
A Prosecutor Dresse reached out
regarding the medical file.

146
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:00,680
He is in Berlin.

147
00:10:00,760 --> 00:10:04,960
Apparently trying to obtain a visa
for the United States.

148
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:09,000
You spoke with him...

149
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:11,800
I only made a phone call,
nothing more.

150
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:15,920
He will meet you.
I noted down the time and place.

151
00:10:16,680 --> 00:10:19,400
Now if you will excuse me,
I have work to do.

152
00:10:19,560 --> 00:10:20,760
*mysterious music*

153
00:10:20,840 --> 00:10:21,880
Thank you.

154
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:35,640
*trap clacks*

155
00:10:36,680 --> 00:10:38,160
*echoing gunshot*

156
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:42,200
Surely you see that you backed
the wrong horse.

157
00:10:42,280 --> 00:10:44,000
So far all I see is you trying

158
00:10:44,080 --> 00:10:46,800
to get me back into your boat,
my dear Herr Schleicher.

159
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:49,000
Yet Hitler cannot be controlled.

160
00:10:50,520 --> 00:10:52,240
Yes, it may seem that way,

161
00:10:52,400 --> 00:10:55,040
yet with money, anyone can be controlled.

162
00:10:55,920 --> 00:10:58,720
Others have fallen victim
to that fallacy before.

163
00:10:59,720 --> 00:11:01,680
Hitler is an enemy of capital.

164
00:11:01,760 --> 00:11:05,080
You were Reich Chancellor once.
You had your chance.

165
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:08,400
This time Hindenburg followed Hitler,
not you.

166
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:10,680
That's democracy for you.

167
00:11:10,760 --> 00:11:13,840
(dismissive) Democracy.
Nyssen, now listen here.

168
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:18,560
What we need is a national-right pact
against Hitler,

169
00:11:18,640 --> 00:11:20,680
with you and the leading industrialists,

170
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:24,120
with the German Nationals,
reasonable men from the NSDAP,

171
00:11:24,200 --> 00:11:25,480
with the military.

172
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,840
And with my money?
Yes, with your money.

173
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:33,640
I promise you,
it is the best investment you'll ever make.

174
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:36,000
That sounds intriguing.

175
00:11:36,840 --> 00:11:38,080
*trap clacks*

176
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:40,840
*echoing gunshot*

177
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:44,720
Congratulations, Director,
a direct hit.

178
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:46,360
Herr Former Reich Chancellor.

179
00:11:47,360 --> 00:11:50,120
What is it?
Your wife asks that you join her.

180
00:11:50,200 --> 00:11:52,680
She has news of the utmost importance.

181
00:11:54,880 --> 00:11:56,280
You will excuse me.

182
00:12:02,080 --> 00:12:03,720
*door opens*

183
00:12:03,880 --> 00:12:05,280
*footsteps*

184
00:12:07,640 --> 00:12:08,760
What is it?

185
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:11,440
I reached Minister Göring.

186
00:12:12,160 --> 00:12:13,160
And?

187
00:12:14,600 --> 00:12:15,800
It's on.

188
00:12:17,520 --> 00:12:19,920
When?
February 27th. Firmly confirmed.

189
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:23,200
What if he fails to show again,
stands us up once more?

190
00:12:23,280 --> 00:12:25,400
It is the first private invitation
he has accepted

191
00:12:25,480 --> 00:12:27,200
since becoming Reich Chancellor.

192
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:29,040
*he inhales with a hiss*

193
00:12:33,680 --> 00:12:35,480
How did old Papen put it?

194
00:12:36,760 --> 00:12:39,160
'I'll push him against the wall
until he squeaks.'

195
00:12:39,240 --> 00:12:40,600
*distant gunshot*

196
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:47,240
Poor Schleicher.

197
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:50,320
I fear nothing will come of us.

198
00:12:50,840 --> 00:12:52,800
*suspensefully pulsing music*

199
00:12:55,560 --> 00:12:57,440
*clamor of voices*

200
00:12:58,320 --> 00:12:59,760
What did she put down for me...

201
00:13:00,360 --> 00:13:03,240
(laughing) Aha. Potatoes.

202
00:13:16,280 --> 00:13:18,080
(Völcker)
Can we not reach an agreement?

203
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:21,280
No, we have already discussed this candidate.

204
00:13:21,920 --> 00:13:23,640
We did not discuss him.
- Yes we did!

205
00:13:23,720 --> 00:13:27,120
No, we are socialists.
We are capable of compromise.

206
00:13:27,200 --> 00:13:29,080
(Helfers) We'll get nowhere like this!

207
00:13:29,160 --> 00:13:31,840
We'll table the candidate list

208
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:33,920
and proceed with?
- (Litten) Roman numeral four.

209
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:36,200
Measures to stabilize democracy.

210
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:37,280
(Helfers) Thank you.

211
00:13:37,440 --> 00:13:40,160
We envision a joint government
of the SPD and KPD...

212
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:41,680
- Just a moment.

213
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:43,720
It is our turn to propose first.

214
00:13:43,800 --> 00:13:45,800
Alternating turns was the agreement.
- Correct.

215
00:13:46,880 --> 00:13:50,080
Fine. Point one, then.

216
00:13:50,160 --> 00:13:53,080
Petition to ban the NSDAP
as an unconstitutional party.

217
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:56,000
That is our point one as well.
- You see, it is possible.

218
00:13:56,160 --> 00:13:57,680
*she hisses*
- Point two.

219
00:13:57,840 --> 00:14:01,040
Nationalization of all corporations
supporting the NSDAP.

220
00:14:01,120 --> 00:14:02,920
The Quandts, the Krupps, the Nyssens.

221
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:04,040
Proceed.

222
00:14:04,200 --> 00:14:05,200
Point three.

223
00:14:05,680 --> 00:14:08,120
Restructuring of the economic system
and the political order

224
00:14:08,280 --> 00:14:10,080
along Soviet lines.
- Never!

225
00:14:10,960 --> 00:14:15,440
We stand for a democratic economic model.

226
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:17,560
Which nobody wants,
because nobody believes in it!

227
00:14:17,720 --> 00:14:20,160
That is why you lose votes with every election!

228
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:23,240
Because governing is harder work
than opposition!

229
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:26,080
Ha!
- One must make compromises.

230
00:14:26,160 --> 00:14:28,560
You are driving the people
into the arms of the NSDAP!

231
00:14:28,640 --> 00:14:29,920
Outrageous!

232
00:14:30,080 --> 00:14:33,440
Order, ladies and gentlemen,
we cannot go on like this.

233
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:35,120
*chaotic roar of voices*

234
00:14:35,640 --> 00:14:37,520
Pull yourselves together now!

235
00:14:37,600 --> 00:14:40,800
Stop bickering!
This is sickening!

236
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:46,560
We cannot proceed this way.

237
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:50,800
If you place your factional interests
ahead of the greater good,

238
00:14:50,880 --> 00:14:52,160
you will perish.

239
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:53,680
And rightly so.

240
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:56,600
People will say the Left failed

241
00:14:56,680 --> 00:14:59,520
to stop Hitler
when they had the chance!

242
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:03,000
Because you are just as stubborn
as your party leaders.

243
00:15:03,080 --> 00:15:04,080
Both of you!

244
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:09,800
(both) But I...
- If you speak now,

245
00:15:09,880 --> 00:15:11,280
you will prove me right.

246
00:15:17,520 --> 00:15:19,760
(Helfers) Where were we?
- (Völcker) Three.

247
00:15:19,840 --> 00:15:21,000
Point four.

248
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:23,760
*tower bell tolls, chairs slide*

249
00:15:26,280 --> 00:15:27,640
Herr Reich Chancellor.

250
00:15:33,480 --> 00:15:34,480
My Führer.

251
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:39,960
Herr Hitler,

252
00:15:41,320 --> 00:15:44,680
how wonderful that you made it
to our home after all.

253
00:15:46,280 --> 00:15:48,080
We have much to discuss.

254
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:49,960
(Annemarie) She called him.

255
00:15:55,480 --> 00:15:56,880
We have much to discuss,

256
00:15:56,960 --> 00:15:59,840
my dear partner in crime.

257
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:02,480
Your wife called this new gentleman.

258
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:05,560
You are letting the reins slip from your hands.

259
00:16:05,640 --> 00:16:08,880
On the contrary, I am taking them up again,
as you can see.

260
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:12,480
Helga supports me,
and I am very grateful to her for it.

261
00:16:13,960 --> 00:16:15,400
Dear Adolf...

262
00:16:22,720 --> 00:16:26,440
Dear Adolf Hitler,
we have much to discuss.

263
00:16:26,600 --> 00:16:28,040
*metallic clatter*

264
00:16:28,120 --> 00:16:30,240
*door creaks open*

265
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:32,320
*lamps hum*

266
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:38,240
*door closes*

267
00:16:43,160 --> 00:16:44,240
Easy now.

268
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:47,160
Drink.

269
00:16:51,400 --> 00:16:53,440
*he swallows, gasping*

270
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:55,240
*he breathes exhaustedly*

271
00:16:55,920 --> 00:16:58,920
What do you want from me?
I can tell you exactly.

272
00:17:00,360 --> 00:17:02,520
I want the medical file
of Lance Corporal Hitler

273
00:17:02,600 --> 00:17:06,000
from his stay in Pasewalk
in November 1918.

274
00:17:07,359 --> 00:17:08,920
*somber music*

275
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:10,359
I know nothing about it.

276
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:14,200
I doubt that.

277
00:17:17,520 --> 00:17:19,880
I took Dr. Forster to Paris.

278
00:17:21,160 --> 00:17:24,920
Not because of any file,
but because he needed protection.

279
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:27,160
From people like you.

280
00:17:28,319 --> 00:17:30,160
You can waste your time

281
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:32,840
and beat whatever you want out of me.

282
00:17:34,320 --> 00:17:37,080
Yet what you are looking for,
you will not find with me.

283
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:39,920
*tense, restless music*

284
00:17:48,520 --> 00:17:49,560
Very well.

285
00:17:58,560 --> 00:18:02,000
Then I wish you the best of luck
in the new German Reich,

286
00:18:03,160 --> 00:18:06,680
as a personal enemy
of Reich Chancellor Adolf Hitler.

287
00:18:07,880 --> 00:18:09,640
*footsteps recede*

288
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:28,840
*agitated clamor of voices*

289
00:18:38,440 --> 00:18:41,840
*SA men shout indistinctly*

290
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:44,160
(SA Man) Langenberger! Camp North!

291
00:18:45,160 --> 00:18:47,800
New arrivals, fall in here!
- Two more from cell 12.

292
00:18:47,880 --> 00:18:49,120
All present.

293
00:18:49,560 --> 00:18:52,360
Fall into line!
- Out now!

294
00:18:52,440 --> 00:18:53,640
Let's move!
- That Heymann?

295
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:55,880
Jakob!
- Cell eight, move.

296
00:18:57,400 --> 00:19:01,360
Katelbach, change of tracks.
Come with us now. Let's go.

297
00:19:02,040 --> 00:19:03,440
(SA Man) Faster.

298
00:19:05,240 --> 00:19:06,240
Move!

299
00:19:08,320 --> 00:19:09,840
All hands!

300
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,360
What am I supposed to do with you now?
- Escape.

301
00:19:12,440 --> 00:19:14,880
Stay with me
and wait for my signal.

302
00:19:14,960 --> 00:19:15,960
Brock!

303
00:19:17,120 --> 00:19:18,640
Let's go!
- Yes.

304
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:19,840
(SA Man) Look sharp!

305
00:19:21,040 --> 00:19:22,200
Mandel!

306
00:19:22,680 --> 00:19:23,720
Eckert!

307
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:26,680
Müllensiefen!

308
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:30,120
Heymann!

309
00:19:30,760 --> 00:19:31,920
Heymann.

310
00:19:33,240 --> 00:19:36,720
I'll take the older one,
you take the kid with the rifle.

311
00:19:36,880 --> 00:19:39,400
Remember, on my command.

312
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,520
(SA Man) Ready up!
- Is everyone inside?

313
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:44,400
Mount up, quickly!

314
00:19:44,560 --> 00:19:46,520
Czerwinski, Henning, depart!

315
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:48,520
Move, roll out.

316
00:19:49,640 --> 00:19:51,600
*suspenseful music*

317
00:19:54,320 --> 00:19:56,760
*indistinct shouting*

318
00:20:04,320 --> 00:20:06,160
(SA Man) Halt! Identification!

319
00:20:09,960 --> 00:20:11,120
All in order.

320
00:20:20,600 --> 00:20:23,960
Henning! Czerwinski!

321
00:20:24,120 --> 00:20:26,000
*he breathes with exertion*

322
00:20:30,800 --> 00:20:31,920
*brakes squeal*

323
00:20:32,080 --> 00:20:33,440
Halt!

324
00:20:33,880 --> 00:20:35,440
Police, step out!

325
00:20:35,600 --> 00:20:37,280
Out of the car!
Hey!

326
00:20:37,440 --> 00:20:39,920
I'm sorry.
You cannot... that's impossible!

327
00:20:41,560 --> 00:20:43,200
(Litten) Someone will be able
to tell me

328
00:20:43,360 --> 00:20:45,600
where my clients
have been taken!

329
00:20:45,760 --> 00:20:49,160
You call me back immediately
when you learn anything, understood?

330
00:20:49,240 --> 00:20:50,320
Thank you!

331
00:20:51,840 --> 00:20:53,720
Where on earth is Katelbach?

332
00:20:55,720 --> 00:20:56,920
*nervously pulsing music*

333
00:20:59,160 --> 00:21:01,080
What is going on here?

334
00:21:05,480 --> 00:21:06,560
Holy...

335
00:21:23,440 --> 00:21:25,400
*music turns more dramatic*

336
00:21:25,560 --> 00:21:26,960
*engine roars*

337
00:21:34,520 --> 00:21:36,040
*engine revs high*

338
00:21:43,360 --> 00:21:44,640
Henning!

339
00:21:45,600 --> 00:21:46,800
*he honks the horn*

340
00:21:48,520 --> 00:21:49,560
Henning! Pull over!

341
00:21:51,080 --> 00:21:52,600
What does that lunatic want?

342
00:21:55,800 --> 00:21:57,200
Pull over!

343
00:21:57,360 --> 00:21:59,200
*horn honks, tires squeal*

344
00:22:01,560 --> 00:22:03,240
*he pants shakily*

345
00:22:03,680 --> 00:22:05,040
What an idiot.

346
00:22:05,200 --> 00:22:06,880
*music turns more restless*

347
00:22:26,120 --> 00:22:27,240
(Heymann) Now.

348
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:30,000
Matern!

349
00:22:30,160 --> 00:22:31,160
Shut your trap!

350
00:22:34,320 --> 00:22:36,640
*music swells dramatically*

351
00:22:56,280 --> 00:22:57,480
*tires screech*

352
00:22:59,440 --> 00:23:00,720
(Heymann) Matern!

353
00:23:00,880 --> 00:23:03,000
Grenadier, pull yourself together!

354
00:23:06,200 --> 00:23:07,760
*gunshot*
- *Heymann screams*

355
00:23:08,720 --> 00:23:09,800
What was that?

356
00:23:09,960 --> 00:23:11,440
*sounds of fighting*

357
00:23:15,200 --> 00:23:16,200
*tires squeal*

358
00:23:16,360 --> 00:23:18,240
*chaotic shouting*

359
00:23:18,320 --> 00:23:19,600
*music fades out*

360
00:23:19,760 --> 00:23:22,080
Don't move!
I'll shoot!

361
00:23:22,240 --> 00:23:24,760
(Langenberger)
Shoot! Shoot, boy!

362
00:23:24,920 --> 00:23:26,080
*loud gunshot*

363
00:23:28,680 --> 00:23:29,920
*she sighs*

364
00:23:34,560 --> 00:23:35,600
Your weapon, quickly.

365
00:23:47,800 --> 00:23:48,800
*hectic breathing*

366
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:50,320
Who are you?

367
00:23:52,280 --> 00:23:53,720
*weary breathing*

368
00:23:55,960 --> 00:23:57,240
*he laughs*

369
00:23:58,760 --> 00:23:59,960
*he groans*

370
00:24:00,680 --> 00:24:02,440
*he gasps for air*

371
00:24:05,920 --> 00:24:07,720
*he speaks indistinctly*

372
00:24:08,200 --> 00:24:12,120
The spuds.
Potatoes, I mean.

373
00:24:12,520 --> 00:24:16,000
She always picks the floury ones.

374
00:24:16,920 --> 00:24:18,600
*Katelbach wheezes faintly*

375
00:24:22,520 --> 00:24:23,640
*Heymann chokes up*

376
00:24:28,360 --> 00:24:30,160
*he breathes shakily*

377
00:24:40,520 --> 00:24:42,960
*mournful violin music*

378
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:18,240
*music turns more tense*

379
00:25:32,560 --> 00:25:34,720
This way no suspicion
will fall on you.

380
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:35,920
Are you sure?

381
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:38,640
Do you have a better idea?

382
00:25:39,280 --> 00:25:41,160
Well, from your mouth to God's ear.

383
00:25:42,440 --> 00:25:43,800
Best of luck, men.

384
00:25:54,240 --> 00:25:55,720
*engine starts*

385
00:26:05,760 --> 00:26:08,160
*mournful violin music continues*

386
00:26:12,560 --> 00:26:14,640
*traffic noise*

387
00:26:24,600 --> 00:26:26,440
*doorbell rings*

388
00:26:31,560 --> 00:26:33,480
*sad piano notes*

389
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:56,120
Frau Behnke,

390
00:26:56,960 --> 00:27:00,920
Katelbach and Heymann were
to be transported to a camp.

391
00:27:01,080 --> 00:27:03,040
*piano drowns out dialogue*

392
00:27:07,080 --> 00:27:08,920
He died in my arms.

393
00:27:14,840 --> 00:27:16,160
You are injured.

394
00:27:17,680 --> 00:27:19,360
Let me help you.

395
00:27:21,680 --> 00:27:23,120
*Litten sobs*

396
00:27:25,760 --> 00:27:28,520
(whispers) And there are more camps
outside the city?

397
00:27:28,600 --> 00:27:32,120
(whispers) Yes. We suspect
in the north, just beyond the city limits.

398
00:27:32,760 --> 00:27:36,160
How many inmates were there?
The more information I have,

399
00:27:36,240 --> 00:27:38,280
the more I can accomplish in court.

400
00:27:38,360 --> 00:27:40,120
In our cell there were twelve men.

401
00:27:40,200 --> 00:27:42,360
In the entire compound,
perhaps 40 cells.

402
00:27:42,440 --> 00:27:44,840
Some of them must have had
double occupancy.

403
00:27:50,560 --> 00:27:52,280
*murmuring*

404
00:28:00,840 --> 00:28:03,720
Frau Völcker, Frau Helfers...

405
00:28:05,600 --> 00:28:08,720
it was a matter close to Herr Katelbach's heart

406
00:28:08,800 --> 00:28:12,680
that your two parties find common ground
in this affair.

407
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:17,840
Therefore I would ask you
to reach a swift resolution.

408
00:28:18,680 --> 00:28:19,960
Would that be possible?

409
00:28:28,440 --> 00:28:30,160
*melancholy piano music*

410
00:28:33,400 --> 00:28:34,880
(Woman) Women for Hitler.

411
00:28:36,200 --> 00:28:37,480
Women for Hitler.

412
00:28:40,040 --> 00:28:42,200
(Berlin dialect) Lotte? Hello there!

413
00:28:43,400 --> 00:28:44,600
Oh, Doris.

414
00:28:45,400 --> 00:28:46,760
Off work already?

415
00:28:48,800 --> 00:28:50,080
How's it going?

416
00:28:50,240 --> 00:28:52,640
I have a new job.
Women for Hitler.

417
00:28:53,640 --> 00:28:55,160
Everything depends on us now.

418
00:28:57,440 --> 00:28:59,800
(Berlin dialect) That over there?
No, that's voluntary.

419
00:28:59,880 --> 00:29:02,480
No, something real. In radio.

420
00:29:03,080 --> 00:29:04,280
Congratulations.

421
00:29:04,360 --> 00:29:06,480
Yes, things are looking up for Germany.

422
00:29:08,960 --> 00:29:11,640
I can see you're not convinced yet.

423
00:29:11,720 --> 00:29:14,040
No, I'm not. Not at all.

424
00:29:14,600 --> 00:29:16,720
You lack an eye for what matters.

425
00:29:16,800 --> 00:29:18,560
I felt the same way in the beginning.

426
00:29:18,640 --> 00:29:21,360
Yet believe me,
you'll see it differently soon.

427
00:29:21,440 --> 00:29:24,600
The Führer is leading us all back home.

428
00:29:24,680 --> 00:29:27,960
(snorts) I can find my own way there,
and right now, in fact.

429
00:29:28,120 --> 00:29:30,800
Your rejection comes from the intellect.

430
00:29:30,960 --> 00:29:34,680
Yet this is about feeling,
about security and love.

431
00:29:34,840 --> 00:29:36,400
Love is the most important thing.

432
00:29:36,920 --> 00:29:39,800
Yes, you may be right about that.
Yes, that's true, Lottchen.

433
00:29:39,880 --> 00:29:42,160
Love,
love for Germany.

434
00:29:44,880 --> 00:29:47,520
Doris, even when you talk nonsense,
I still love you.

435
00:29:48,920 --> 00:29:50,840
Take care.
You too, Lottchen.

436
00:29:53,200 --> 00:29:55,080
Sooner or later, you too will find

437
00:29:55,240 --> 00:29:57,400
faith in a national awakening.

438
00:29:57,480 --> 00:29:59,520
*departure whistle*

439
00:30:00,400 --> 00:30:02,680
*wistful string music*

440
00:30:04,160 --> 00:30:05,440
*she snorts*

441
00:30:39,040 --> 00:30:40,800
*engine starts*

442
00:30:49,120 --> 00:30:50,280
Charlotte?

443
00:31:21,600 --> 00:31:23,120
*he sighs wearily*

444
00:31:39,360 --> 00:31:40,640
*match hisses*

445
00:31:40,800 --> 00:31:42,080
*faint thumping*

446
00:32:00,960 --> 00:32:02,720
*he groans and wheezes*

447
00:32:09,600 --> 00:32:11,000
*he wheezes*

448
00:32:13,120 --> 00:32:14,480
*sounds of struggle*

449
00:32:22,200 --> 00:32:23,560
*Döbrich groans*

450
00:32:29,480 --> 00:32:31,360
*agonized screams*

451
00:32:48,800 --> 00:32:50,720
*SA man groans in pain*

452
00:32:54,480 --> 00:32:55,840
*he breathes frantically*

453
00:33:10,280 --> 00:33:11,480
*he breathes shakily*

454
00:33:29,800 --> 00:33:31,480
*he gasps for air*

455
00:33:36,400 --> 00:33:38,280
*distant dog barking*

456
00:33:38,440 --> 00:33:39,600
*gunshot*

457
00:33:41,960 --> 00:33:43,280
*Döbrich groans*

458
00:33:50,120 --> 00:33:51,760
*SA man collapses to the floor*

459
00:34:01,560 --> 00:34:02,840
*gunshot*

460
00:34:05,440 --> 00:34:07,040
*he breathes in panic*

461
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:11,000
Charlotte.

462
00:34:12,199 --> 00:34:13,199
Gereon.

463
00:34:25,120 --> 00:34:26,239
Döbrich.

464
00:34:33,480 --> 00:34:34,520
Who is that?

465
00:34:36,840 --> 00:34:37,920
Where were you?

466
00:34:38,440 --> 00:34:40,639
Who are these men?
Where were you?

467
00:34:40,719 --> 00:34:42,840
Charlotte.
Gereon, where were you?

468
00:34:45,120 --> 00:34:46,880
No word! Nothing!

469
00:34:46,960 --> 00:34:48,800
Stop it!
I was worried sick!

470
00:34:48,960 --> 00:34:50,600
Just listen to me!

471
00:34:51,239 --> 00:34:52,280
No!

472
00:34:53,159 --> 00:34:56,000
You want to blow something up
and you make me an accomplice,

473
00:34:56,080 --> 00:34:58,000
without saying a single word to me!

474
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:01,440
I've had it!
Charlotte.

475
00:35:01,920 --> 00:35:04,480
I am so sick of not being able to trust you.

476
00:35:05,560 --> 00:35:07,120
How do you know this man?

477
00:35:11,040 --> 00:35:12,480
Works at the Burg.

478
00:35:16,520 --> 00:35:18,200
We have to get out of here.

479
00:35:21,080 --> 00:35:24,600
Charlotte, please.
We cannot stay here.

480
00:35:27,280 --> 00:35:28,600
Where are we supposed to go?

481
00:35:42,320 --> 00:35:43,760
*loud knocking*

482
00:35:43,920 --> 00:35:45,240
Police, open up!

483
00:35:48,800 --> 00:35:50,320
*doorbell rings*

484
00:35:55,280 --> 00:35:57,560
Yes? Did something happen?

485
00:35:58,120 --> 00:35:59,880
The neighbors report there was noise.

486
00:36:00,320 --> 00:36:02,040
Here?
Well...

487
00:36:02,960 --> 00:36:06,400
There were loud bangs!
And crashing like mad.

488
00:36:09,160 --> 00:36:13,080
I drew a bath.
The water always splashes so loudly.

489
00:36:16,960 --> 00:36:20,000
Would you like to step in for a moment?
I'll make us some coffee.

490
00:36:20,640 --> 00:36:22,280
*constable clears throat*

491
00:36:22,440 --> 00:36:25,720
No, no, young lady.
Enjoy your bath in peace.

492
00:36:26,360 --> 00:36:29,560
A shootout looks quite different,
ladies and gentlemen, please.

493
00:36:29,720 --> 00:36:31,560
Move along now!
Good day.

494
00:36:38,280 --> 00:36:39,920
*gentle piano music*

495
00:37:02,960 --> 00:37:04,840
*music turns darker*

496
00:37:25,280 --> 00:37:26,880
Where is Dr. Forster?

497
00:37:27,880 --> 00:37:28,880
In Paris.

498
00:37:30,000 --> 00:37:32,240
Arrested?
No.

499
00:37:34,160 --> 00:37:35,880
They only caught me.

500
00:37:39,720 --> 00:37:41,600
Why didn't you ever reach out?

501
00:37:44,720 --> 00:37:46,920
I did. Several times.

502
00:37:47,080 --> 00:37:48,760
*she clicks her tongue in disbelief*

503
00:37:52,520 --> 00:37:53,520
Charlotte, I...

504
00:37:54,080 --> 00:37:55,720
*his breath catches*

505
00:38:26,800 --> 00:38:28,480
*stately piano music*

506
00:38:41,920 --> 00:38:44,200
*wistful violin music*

507
00:38:46,000 --> 00:38:47,000
*

